sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-09> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because n cells sharing a common outer boundary and meeting three at a time need n − 3 intermediate partitions, every possible grouping of eight cells all reaching the surface belongs to one of thirteen topological types, so all drawings of such eight-celled structures represent one of these types.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "All surface groupings of eight cells belong to one of thirteen types";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "n cells meeting three at a time with a common outer boundary need n - 3 partitions; n = 8 gives 13 types";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Thirteen types of eight-celled groups”. Scope: eight-celled aggregates of plants and animals with all cells at the surface (segmenting eggs, apical cones, discs)"
}
